// Verifying BLS signatures — four entry points, five infinity guards, and one of the five is
// observable.
//
// `packages/bls/src/verify.wac` did the analysis this directory usually has to do, by hand, and
// wrote the answer down:
//
// > An infinity public key means a zero secret key. Refused outright — but **not** because the
// > Ethereum fixtures require it, which is what this said before somebody deleted the line and
// > watched them all still pass. … The **one** infinity guard in this file that a test can see is
// > `aggregatePubkeys`'s … Deleting this one, or `aggregateVerify`'s per-key copy, changes no
// > fixture … All three stay: defence in depth is the point, and now the comments say which is
// > load-bearing.
//
// That is `@/packages/lightclient`'s finding — *an unobservable check is unobservable because the
// answer is a `bool`* — with a second witness, and one difference that matters: **this author ran
// the mutation.** lightclient's file says deleting any of three checks changes no verdict and stops
// there; this one deleted the line, watched the fixtures pass, and kept the guard with a note.
//
// So the claim can be tested rather than asserted, and it holds.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec } from "core";
import { Verified } from "@/packages/crypto";

/** The key does not decode to a G1 point. */
export struct BadPublicKey { i32 which; }
/** The signature does not decode to a G2 point. */
export struct BadSignature { }
/** A public key is the identity, which is a zero secret key. */
export struct InfinityPublicKey { i32 which; }
/** The signature is the identity. */
export struct InfinitySignature { }
/** No keys, or the lists are different lengths. */
export struct WrongArity { i32 keys; i32 messages; i32 signatures; }
/** The pairing equation does not hold. The answer. */

export union<BadPublicKey, BadSignature, InfinityPublicKey, InfinitySignature, WrongArity>
  BlsFault;

/** One signer. `e(pk, H(m)) == e(G₁, sig)`. */
export Result<Verified, BlsFault> verify(Bytes pubkey, Bytes message, Bytes signature) { … }

/** Many signers, one message — the keys sum, so it is one verification plus n−1 G1 additions. */
export Result<Verified, BlsFault> fastAggregateVerify(Vec<Bytes> pubkeys, Bytes message, Bytes sig) { … }

/** Many signers, distinct messages — the keys cannot sum, so the pairings combine. */
export Result<Verified, BlsFault> aggregateVerify(Vec<Bytes> pubkeys, Vec<Bytes> messages, Bytes sig) { … }

/** Many signatures at once, randomised so an adversary cannot split the terms. */
export Result<Verified, BlsFault> batchVerify(
  Vec<Bytes> pubkeys, Vec<Bytes> messages, Vec<Bytes> signatures, Bytes entropy) { … }

// ## 1. Four of the five guards become observable, and the fifth already was
//
// The shipped file establishes, by deletion, that only `aggregatePubkeys`'s infinity check changes a
// fixture. The reason the others cannot is arithmetic: `pk = O` reduces the identity to
// `e(−G1, sig) == 1`, which holds only for `sig = O`, and the infinity-*signature* guard answers
// first. So the guard is right and no test can see it.
//
// With named refusals it can. Delete `verify`'s `g1IsInfinity(pk)` and an infinity key reaches the
// pairing, which fails, so the answer is `Ok(Invalid)` where it was `Err(InfinityPublicKey)`.
// **Two different answers, so the mutation is caught** — by a test that asserts *which* refusal,
// which is a test somebody has to write and which a `bool` gives no way to write at all.
//
// And since 2026-09-05 the two differ by more than a name: one is an `Ok` and one is an `Err`, so
// the mutation moves the answer across the `Result` boundary and a caller that only wrote `try`
// notices. §3.
//
// That is lightclient's claim, and here it is checkable against a case where the unobservability was
// measured rather than argued. Five guards: one observable today, five observable with a named
// refusal, and the cost is a union of six members in a package that already has six ways to fail.
//
// **What it does not buy**, and the shipped file is careful about this: the guards are *defence in
// depth*, so making them observable does not make them necessary. Four of the five are still
// arithmetically redundant. What changes is that a later edit that removes one has to remove a test
// with it, rather than passing quietly.
//
// ## 3. `DoesNotVerify` was a member of the union and is the `Ok` now
//
// It was `union<BadPublicKey, BadSignature, InfinityPublicKey, InfinitySignature, WrongArity,
// DoesNotVerify>`, so *the signature does not check out* — the ordinary answer of a verifier —
// arrived through `Err`. `@/packages/crypto/src/ed25519.wac` had the same member, with a doc comment
// calling it *"the honest answer, and the only one of the six that is about the signature"*, which is
// the argument for it not being there, written next to it being there.
//
// **Two packages, one union member, both wrong the same way**, and the thing that found it was a
// duplicate-name sweep: `BadPublicKey` and `DoesNotVerify` are each declared in both files.
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s rule from that sweep — *a duplicate name is the cheapest signal that two
// files have solved one problem twice* — and this is the case where the second thing found was much
// larger than the first.
//
// `verify` answers `Result<Verified, BlsFault>` now, `Verified` lives in `@/packages/crypto`, and the
// consequence is not cosmetic: a caller writing `try verify(…)` used to propagate **signature
// invalid** as an error indistinguishable from **this is not a signature**. For a consensus client
// those want opposite responses — drop the message, or stop trusting the peer.
//
// ## 2. Four entry points, four refusal sets, and no way to compare them
//
// `verify`, `fastAggregateVerify`, `aggregateVerify` and `batchVerify` refuse in 3, 3, 9 and 9
// places, and whether they agree cannot be read off any one of them. The interesting case is
// `fastAggregateVerify`, which does **not** check that the aggregate is infinity, where `verify`
// checks its single key:
//
//   * `aggregatePubkeys` refuses any *input* key that is infinity, so the fixture case is covered;
//   * two valid keys can still sum to infinity — `k` and `−k` — and that aggregate reaches the
//     pairing;
//   * and it cannot verify, because `e(O, H(m)) = 1` leaves `e(−G₁, sig) == 1`, which needs
//     `sig = O`, which `g2IsInfinity(sig)` already refused.
//
// So it is safe, and the reasoning spans three functions and one algebraic identity. **Not a bug —
// checked — and the shape is the one this directory keeps meeting**: safe because of what something
// else happens to do, with nothing relating the two. `@/packages/zstd`'s `Buf` invariant is the same
// sentence about a different subject.
//
// A `Result` does not fix that either. What would is the thing the file already does and nothing
// else in the tree does: **write down which guard is load-bearing, having tested it by deletion.**
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`WrongArity` carries three counts because four functions share one fault type**, and only
// `batchVerify` has three lists. `verify` can never produce it. That is a union member that is
// unreachable from three of its four producers, which is the cost of one error type per package
// rather than per function — and the alternative, four unions, makes a caller that dispatches over
// entry points hold four types for one question. `@/packages/http` chose the first and so does this;
// nothing has written the trade-off down.
//
// **A signature and a public key are both `Bytes` and are 96 and 48 bytes.** Passing them in the
// wrong order type-checks and answers `BadPublicKey`, which is correct and misleading. Seventh
// package wanting a length in a type, and the first where the two lengths are *different* and the
// error is still not a compile error.
//
// **Randomisation is a parameter and its quality is not.** `batchVerify` takes `entropy` as `Bytes`,
// and the whole security of batching rests on an adversary not predicting it. A caller passing
// `u8[32]()` gets a batch verifier that accepts invalid signatures. There is no type for *this came
// from a `Random` capability*, which is `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s capability question arriving as a
// **value that must have a provenance** — the fourth *guarantee from how it was made*, and the first
// where the guarantee is about an argument rather than a result.
